04 #edb/ref 17592186045717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1007 "Mathematical Foundations II" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1008 "Mathematical Foundations II is the second course in a sequence specially crafted for adults seeking the most direct path to prepare for university math courses. Building on the basic algebra covered in Mathematical Foundations I , students in Mathematical Foundations II generalize their understanding of algebra to more advanced functions (including trigonometry) and become acquainted with limits and derivatives in calculus. Upon completing the course, students will be prepared for Mathematical Foundations III , the final stepping stone in preparing for university-level math." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1009 "Master the algebra of advanced functions including quadratics, logarithms, and trigonometry. Dive deep into the theory of polynomials, learn the basics of limits, derivatives, and integrals from calculus, and explore a variety of concepts from higher math including complex numbers, vectors, probability, and statistics." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1012 111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1013 "MF2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "b5d8b420-892a-5b6a-8150-ba3c70c4d779" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048186 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048186 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048186 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048186 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048186 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048186 #edb/ref 1031 "Combining Graph Transformations: Three or More Operations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048186 #edb/ref 1033 634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048186 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "869fb66c-d668-58cf-9dd4-b10651dbe9d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048753 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048753 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048753 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048753 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048753 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048753 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048753 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048753 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048753 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048753 #edb/ref 1031 "Combining Reflections With Other Graph Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048753 #edb/ref 1033 2841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048753 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "bb8df0ff-58fd-5ef3-8af3-6a8fd08c46ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048796 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186184193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048796 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186184227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048796 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186184234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048796 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186184235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048796 #edb/ref 1031 "Constructing Functions Using Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048796 #edb/ref 1033 3845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048796 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "bfab9d57-0ef9-5cb2-ad40-9f6fefe847aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050085 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050085 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050085 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050085 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050085 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050085 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050085 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186100886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050085 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186100946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050085 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186100947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050085 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186100948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050085 #edb/ref 1031 "Combining Graph Transformations: Two Operations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050085 #edb/ref 1033 1254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050085 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "2c0b4af5-6010-5e83-8fcc-56c647d1ade5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078360 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7622" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078360 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of $y=f\\left(\\dfrac x2-3\\right)+5$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\textrm{T}1)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n- $(\\textrm{T}2)$ - Translate up by $5$ units\n- $(\\textrm{T}3)$ - Translate right by $6$ units\n- $(\\textrm{T}4)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $\\dfrac 12$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\nWhat is a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078360 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bbd2751e-73b2-500b-abef-c67564a9559f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078360 #edb/ref 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n2. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(\\dfrac x2-3\\right)+5 = f\\left(\\dfrac 12(x-6)\\right)+5\n$$\n\nTo get\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(\\dfrac 12(x-6)\\right)+5\n$$\n\nwe could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x)$.\n- Apply transformation $(\\textrm{T}1)$: Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f\\left(\\dfrac x2 \\right)$.\n- Apply transformation $(\\textrm{T}3)$: Translate $y=f\\left(\\dfrac x2 \\right)$ right by $6$ units to get the graph of $y=f\\left(\\dfrac12{(x-6)}\\right)$.\n- Apply transformation $(\\textrm{T}2)$: Translate $y=f\\left(\\dfrac12{(x-6)}\\right)$ up by $5$ units to get the graph of $y=f\\left(\\dfrac12{(x-6)}\\right)+5$.\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$$\n(\\textrm{T}1) \\to (\\textrm{T}3) \\to (\\textrm{T}2)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078361 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7623" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078361 #edb/ref 1071 "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x)$. Plot the graph of $y=f\\left(2x+2\\right)-1$.\n\n![](images/69/69e2a7112819db4536ea902c236c7d272fb8fb5ada1d114a59dcea5de212d4d5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078361 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7dbe419-31b2-5c5a-bcab-738aa6fd46fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078361 #edb/ref 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n2. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny = f\\left(2x+2\\right) -1 = f\\left(2(x+1)\\right) - 1\n$$\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(2(x+1)\\right) - 1\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x)$.\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(2x)$.\n\n![](images/b9/b9f7d5c0b8014bc3e37171efb5bcc6422573cb4dc3a3a3ed783ca10385fd9a86.png)\n\n- Then, translate it to the left by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2(x+1)\\right)$.\n\n![](images/d4/d4e0469b84d60010a5cd78ee9b575aec441180b615a2db5587099d65e4bf3496.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it down by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2(x+1)\\right)-1$.\n\n![](images/c6/c6ebcaa8fd8d421de53231e381130967ec35a270b35786d17a0e4c9e98420a9e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078362 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7624" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078362 #edb/ref 1071 "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x)$. Plot the graph of $y=2f\\left(3(x+3)\\right)+5$.\n\n![](images/b3/b314de8320a82ad5a7bd395da63c6f9bd4348ee2f4fd12c13a090c20fbcdd322.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078362 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6ee9bee5-8027-5ba5-ad2c-9b697f2affc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078362 #edb/ref 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n2. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny=2f\\left({3}(x+3)\\right)+5\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x)$.\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f\\left({3}x\\right)$.\n\n![](images/92/92ffae3729220d6eae6cfb96fde2efc93f19b58ad38a5ba36760fb8b38ee8ced.png)\n\n- Then, translate it left by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=f\\left({3}(x+3)\\right)$.\n\n![](images/c4/c4fc767f318d276a0178f4ceb2d2550a5141744ca2c3ed8da1f240309d3a1ca0.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2f\\left(3(x+3)\\right)$.\n\n![](images/14/143a49c724c30a9e9cf432960fcf7046784a9a881182959875b9805adb0a8c5e.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it up by $5$ units to get the graph of $y=2f\\left(3(x+3)\\right)+5$.\n\n![](images/6d/6d7295138c2821d0d3c479ff9d98e2018bc26493ff09f41fe463cc31cee0907e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078510 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078510 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078510 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Correct Order of Transformations Given a Transformed Function Expression" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078510 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078510 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2c2387a4-202d-528a-b0ca-3f87d5a06ea3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078511 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078511 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078511 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078511 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Graph Transformations Resulting From Three Operations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078511 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078511 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "17339421-a1ad-554f-aed8-28e20cebab10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078512 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078512 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078512 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Graph Transformations Resulting From Four Operations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078512 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078512 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d69a3ef2-0854-503b-8586-b0113966d9ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078658 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078658 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078658 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078658 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078658 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078658 #edb/ref 1045 "Combining Graph Transformations: Three or More Operations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078658 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186079423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078658 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078658 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ff014b49-75da-5266-b4b5-9e7bd3e5b3d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079423 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079423 #edb/ref 1061 28409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079423 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e6e20122-6866-5fbc-9527-7a17b8c290e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079423 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079424 #edb/ref 1061 28411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079424 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a78dc881-a3e2-5e40-bd1b-cf28fe897545" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079424 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079425 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079425 #edb/ref 1061 28412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079425 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a6368e4f-8808-5bd9-b6a8-0a2f253f3384" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079425 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079426 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079426 #edb/ref 1061 28413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079426 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6856acd9-d60c-536e-80e4-da84599cde69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079426 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079675 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we are given the graph of $y=f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/2c/2cb61ee785ff6e04c5b73281389d6653f42bc93636da6840fbc2391ffb82451c.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$, how can we use graph transformations to plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(2x-4\\right)+1 \\,\n$$\n\nIn this particular situation, we have to combine several operations.\n\nTo perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must apply the following rule:\n\n1. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n2. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nIn this case, we begin by factoring out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny = f\\left(2x-4\\right) +1 = f\\left(2(x-2)\\right) + 1\n$$\n\nNow, to plot\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(2(x-2)\\right) +1\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x)$.\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(2x)$.\n\n![](images/07/07fe84bddbdc721dfba2fc9b720ca79ff794e9846fa1ef3bbc0c3c2217edd4e8.png)\n\n- Then, translate it to the right by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2(x-2)\\right)$.\n\n![](images/a1/a16ccd8e56c64f80f93476f2b7a41aa3093a2e4b9fdbe089fa502d1f1df15923.png)\n\n- There is no vertical scaling, so we omit this step.\n- Finally, translate it up by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2(x-2)\\right)+1$.\n\n![](images/f4/f4a894860fadbc3ac88fa360cae23ef0f33903f3d930cb00496bb2c2505d2334.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079675 #edb/ref 1065 4616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079675 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bf539881-a530-50df-b39d-b9718839371f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079675 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
